| **i102m** | i102 **sha256 throughput rewrite for the firmware-verify path** (finding, autonomous-loop 2026-06-16). `sha256_compress` (the per-64-byte-block core) costs **~730k T-states/block** — measured 3,648,978 T for the 256-byte baseline = 5 blocks, *after* i102b's −29% rotation pass. That is **~an order of magnitude or more slower than a register-oriented Z80 sha256** (~15–50k T/block): the round + message-schedule loops express every 32-bit operation as a memory-to-memory **subroutine call** (`sha_add4`/`sha_copy4`/`sha_ch`/`sha_maj`/`sha_bigsigma0/1`/`sha_sigma0/1`, each a djnz loop + `call`/`ret`; ~2.5–3k calls/block). Unlike x25519 (the i88 handshake/**stretch**), sha256 is on the **real-deliverable path**: `tcp_conn`'s streaming sink hashes the firmware body through `sha256_update` as it streams (`conn_verify`, i100/q15, `tcp_conn.asm:458`) — so for a multi-MB firmware (`start.elf` ~3 MB) the on-SAM verify is potentially **~hours**. A register-oriented rewrite of `sha256_compress` (working vars in registers / a fixed scratch layout, round function folded inline) could cut it **~10–40×**. Caveats: a substantial, careful rewrite (NIST KAT byte-exact is the guardrail); code-size / **q17 composite-budget**-sensitive (sha256 is in the tls_client composite, ~1.1 KB clearance to `qsq_table`). A helper-inline (`sha_add4`/`sha_copy4` only, ~3%) is the budget-safe down-payment but would be discarded by a full rewrite — so left undone pending the steer. | OPEN — umbrella; q21 resolved (do the rewrite). Increments **i102n–i102r** landed (closed registry): sha256 **729,795 → 315,355 T/block (−56.8%)** — the fastest verified Z80 SHA-256 by a wide margin. **i102r (register-accumulator round, −8.0%, PR #377) was the last sizeable architectural lever** — and a win on both axes (the more compact sequence also shrank the firmware-fetch bootable by 478 B). **RECALIBRATION (autonomous-loop 2026-06-17): the "~112k reference floor" is NOT grounded** in any real implementation — it contradicts q21's own bake-off finding (the whole field is ~480–730k; no faster *verified* Z80 SHA-256 exists; the cited "45k" was fabricated AI code). First-principles cost analysis (round loop ≈62% of the block, message schedule ≈33%; the Z80 7-GP-register wall forces one 32-bit operand to RAM and the bit-granular σ/Σ rotates are inherently multi-byte) caps practically-capturable further gains at **single-digit %**, not the ~3× the 112k implied. **Remaining levers — all small / diminishing:** the message-schedule extend loop still does its 3 term adds memory-to-memory (`add4_body`); applying the same `BCDE +=` accumulate there is **≈2% more** — the one clean lever left. Beyond it: deep diminishing returns (lookup-table rotates ≈6% but cost ≥512 B of tables = a q17-budget hit). No-deadline fun-exercise and we are already world-best, so further grinding is **LOW priority** — bank the schedule ≈2% if convenient, **do not chase the phantom 112k**. | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/`; `src/netboot/tcp_conn.asm` (`conn_verify`); i100, i101 (firmware-verify path); i102n, i102o, i102p, i102q, i102r; q21; i102 |
| **i103** | **Autonomous-loop monitor — activity-gated quiescence** (stop drained-backlog nudges without going deaf; Pete, 2026-06-15). **Problem:** when the non-Pete backlog is genuinely drained, the monitor's ~30-min hang-detection keeps waking an idle session; each wake costs a model turn ("nothing to do") for zero progress. **Fix (Pete's design):** on confirming a genuine drain at a clean turn boundary, the agent writes a **quiescence semaphore** (e.g. `$SEMA_DIR/idle-confirmed`) telling the monitor to stop nudging — but the quiescence is **only valid while the session stays inactive**, so it can't strand the loop. **Mechanism:** when the monitor first observes the quiescence file it records the session transcript's current position (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<session>.jsonl` size/mtime); each poll, while the file exists *and* the transcript has not grown past that mark, the monitor stays **silent** (zero nudges, zero tokens); the instant the transcript grows (Pete writes back, or any new turn), the monitor **deletes the quiescence file itself** and resumes normal monitoring. So "leave me in peace" auto-expires on the first sign of life — no manual restart, no risk of a permanently-deaf loop. **Subtlety:** the agent writes the file as its last action, so the transcript may flush just after — record the transcript mark *when the monitor first sees the file* (not the file's own mtime), or add a small grace window, to avoid a spurious immediate re-wake. Agent side dovetails with the `feedback_autonomous_loop` drained-terminal note (confirm drain → write file → end turn). Builds on **i97**. | OPEN — 📋 captured (Pete 2026-06-15) — design agreed; activity signal = session `.jsonl` growth; auto-re-arms on new activity so it can't strand the loop; **non-blocking** enhancement | i97; q14; `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh`; `feedback_autonomous_loop` memory |
| **i104** | **Per-session retro reports** (Pete, 2026-06-15). At wind-down each session writes a short, blameless retrospective to `~/claude-retro/` (outside the repo) on genuine process/workflow friction, **written independently** — not reading other sessions' reports first — so that converging signal across sessions is real, not echoed. The one rule: *say what genuinely happened — no manufactured problems, no suppressed ones*; **"nothing notable" is a valid, good report**; each friction grounded in a specific incident (the project's cite-your-evidence discipline). Reviewed by hand every few sessions / at milestone close → recurring frictions become actionable changes (CLAUDE.md / memory / a `qN`), and each round checks whether the *previous* round's fixes worked. No aggregation tooling until reports accumulate (don't build the analysis machine before the data). Distinct from the diary (`~/claude-diary/`, freeform personal continuity). | OPEN — 🔨 in progress — practice STARTED 2026-06-15 (`~/claude-retro/README.md` + report #1 written); the wind-down step is wired into the ROADMAP hygiene ritual (this PR); manual review in a few days | `~/claude-retro/README.md`; `docs/ROADMAP.md` §Session hygiene; `feedback_autonomous_loop` memory |
| **i106** | **SAM-as-terminal: an SSH (or telnet) client to reach a host — Pete's Mac — over the physical LAN** (Pete, 2026-06-16; a fun daily-driver direction, **usability is the priority**). The i88 TLS work built the reusable crypto: a SAM SSH client reuses **ChaCha20 + Poly1305 + SHA-256 directly** (modern SSH defaults = `curve25519-sha256` KEX + `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` cipher + an SHA-256-based KDF), riding the i70 **`tcp_conn`** transport. **Usability levers (Pete's framing — trust the physical cable, cheapen computation):** (1) **KEX stub à la Konamiman** — send the constant X25519 basepoint (`u=9`) as the client ephemeral pubkey and use the server's *received* pubkey directly as the shared secret (exploits `X25519(server_priv, basepoint) = server_pub`, so **zero EC math on the SAM** — the ~10-min real-X25519 handshake collapses to ~instant, and it still interoperates with stock OpenSSH `sshd`). Cost: no confidentiality (a handshake-capturing LAN sniffer derives the keys) — acceptable on a trusted direct link. Caveat: confirm OpenSSH accepts the basepoint as a client ephemeral key (Konamiman's secp256r1 analogue works against real servers). (2) **Skip host-key verification** — drops Ed25519 + SHA-512 entirely (the one genuinely-new crypto SSH would otherwise force), fine on a trusted cable. (3) **Rekey** — `RekeyLimit none` on the Mac (and with the KEX stub a rekey is free anyway) → no mid-session ~10-min stalls. After those, the only cost is per-byte ChaCha20-Poly1305 (~a few hundred B/s — usable for SAM **MODE 3 = 64×24**: a full redraw ≈ 5 s, typing instant). A **secure-mode toggle** (real X25519, ~10-min connect) is nearly free to offer since the i88 X25519 code exists. **Telnet alternative:** minimal SAM code (TCP + the telnet option dance, no crypto), but modern macOS ships no `telnetd` (install via Homebrew `inetutils`, bind+firewall to the link interface); plaintext. **Either path:** restrict to the physical link (bind + macOS firewall) so it is never internet-reachable. New SAM work = the SSH packet framing / state machine **or** a telnet client; the crypto primitives (SSH path) are already done. | OPEN — 💡 future daily-driver idea (Pete 2026-06-16); not scheduled; reuses i88 crypto + i70 `tcp_conn`; usability-first — **KEX-stub SSH the sweet spot** (instant, uses the Mac's built-in sshd, looks like real SSH), telnet the minimal-code fallback | i88 (reusable crypto: `src/netboot/chacha20.asm`/`poly1305.asm`/`sha256.asm`/`x25519.asm`); i70 (`src/netboot/tcp_conn.asm`); `docs/specs/tls13-handshake.md` §Prior art (the Konamiman trick); `future_roadmap` + `project_personal_pi_dev_machine` memory |
