| **i111** | **Memory-read coverage / generic dead-code finder** (Pete's idea, 2026-06-17). Instrument the koron-go/z80 harness to record every byte *read* — opcode fetch **and** data load — while running the whole test corpus, then report bytes inside a build's code/data image that were never touched. Each unread region is a **candidate**, not a verdict: either genuinely dead (→ delete, reclaim the scarce code window) or a **coverage gap** (→ add a fixture). Memory is tight (the 12 KB code window, the release-gate `&C000` budget) and dead code has accreted before (i109; i73-L9/q20 sweeps), so a standing, *generic* finder earns its keep. **Phase 1 (easy, immediate): the flat-memory standalone payloads** (`asmparse`, the netboot Z80 routines) — koron-go/z80 is a flat 64 KB Z80, so a read-bitmap hook over `mac.Read`/opcode-fetch + the `.map` symbol ranges is a small, paging-free win. **Phase 2 (the design wrinkle): the paged boot/release binary** — the SAM maps different *physical* pages (ENCTAB via the COMET trampoline; pages 12-15 for tests) onto the same 16-bit logical address, so the trace must record `(physical page, offset)` and hook the paging port, not conflate logical addresses. **False-positive handling (Pete's question — drift-proof): anchor known-acceptable-unread ranges to MARKER SYMBOLS read from the pyz80 `.map`, never fixed addresses** — e.g. wrap an intentionally-uncovered block in `cov_ignore_*_start`/`cov_ignore_*_end` labels (with a one-line reason comment), or exclude a whole named table; symbol-relative ranges move with the code as it grows/shrinks, so re-runs don't re-flag accounted-for regions. The finder reports `(code+data image) − (read-set) − (excluded-symbol-ranges)`. Could later become a soft CI signal, but starts as a diagnostic. LOWER priority than the i48c parser port. | OPEN — 🧭 designed (idea captured; phase-1 flat payloads first) | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/` (harness); `tools/z80-test-harness-go/`; i109, i73 (dead-code precedents); the `.map`/`.sym` build outputs |
| **i112** | **Restore the SAM opening screen that Colin's Trinity boot-ROM patch tramples** (Pete's idea, 2026-06-17). Colin Piggot's custom ROM boots straight from the Trinity interface (no B-DOS floppy needed) but overwrites the normal power-on opening screen. Restore it by appending code to the end of B-DOS (which lives in the Trinity flash) that redraws the opening screen after B-DOS loads. **Scoping (from the stock ROM v3.0 disassembly):** the opening screen is **not** a clean re-callable subroutine — it is the inline "RAINBOW SCREEN" block at ROM `&ED1B` (`docs/sam/sam-coupe_rom-v3.0_annotated-disassembly.txt`): set MODE 3, init `PALTAB` from `INITCOLS` (`&FD51`), build the `LINICOLS` line-interrupt rainbow table (the `RBOWL` loop, ~11-scanline stride, `&FF` terminator), then print the `MILES GORDON TECHNOLOGY plc / © 1990 SAM Coupe` banner (`RST 08H; DB 50H`, UMVAL msg 0 at `&F5DD`). So restoration means **reproducing that draw** in appended Z80 (a faithful port of `&ED1B`–`&ED38`), not calling a routine. **Dependencies / blockers:** (1) **downstream of i87** — we must first dump+diff Colin's patched ROM to know exactly what it tramples and whether `RST 08/DB 50H` + `MODET` still behave under it; (2) **hardware-gated** — appending to B-DOS in Trinity flash + testing the redraw is real-Trinity-only (no Trinity flash/ENC in the harness); (3) touches the **non-redistributable B-DOS 1.5t Trinity fork** — neutral committed-doc citations only, and modifying Colin's B-DOS may warrant his input (ask-Colin is Pete's call); (4) whether there is spare room at the end of B-DOS in the flash is unknown until i87. | OPEN — 🧭 idea captured; **blocked on i87** (patched-ROM dump+diff) then hardware | `docs/sam/sam-coupe_rom-v3.0_annotated-disassembly.txt` (`&ED1B` rainbow/banner); i87 (ROM dump+diff — prerequisite); `~/sam-archive/bdos/analysis/`; `memory/project_sam_community_contacts` (Colin) |
