| **i114** | **Netboot serve manifest + record-allocation strategy** (design 2026-06-18, Pete). The serve-time *name→record* layer the i93 "store walk" left hand-wavy. A **manifest** — a file on the *booted* record (leveraging the Trinity's `RECORD`-then-`BOOT` model, so the server's own disk is already the current drive when it runs) — maps full TFTP names/paths → internal B-DOS records, **decoupling the served namespace from B-DOS's 10-char / flat / restricted-charset filename limits** (which real Pi names — `bcm2711-rpi-400.dtb`, `kernel8.img`, `overlays/…`, `<serial>/…` — break). Entries are **local** (on the boot disk) or **remote** (span records elsewhere) carrying {locator(s), span count, size, optional SHA-256}; resolution is exact-string-match → the existing Resolve/SpanPlan path. Adds a **persisted storage-allocation strategy** in a manifest header (first-free / fixed-list-warns-on-overflow / highest-free / extensible; pack-into-already-claimed-records; **never silently steal a record**). Supports all-in-one / self-contained-project / reusable-server-disk / 3-way-split / **multi-project (same TFTP name → a different blob per project) with shared-files-by-reference**. Design: `docs/specs/netboot-storage-manifest-design.md`. | OPEN — 🔨 design + the 4 sub-choices settled (q26 ✅: text / number+name / hash-derived / highest-free) + **storage-class** settled (Pete 2026-06-18, spec §6.5: explicit `trinity-sam-disks/` prefix → a validated raw-record disk [size==819200 + `BDOS`@232], default = generic flat-file archive; raw-record is bootable *and* servable-raw so no double-copy); the host-verifiable slice (manifest parse + resolve + allocation logic, Go authority + Z80) is buildable; the RST-8 `HRECORD`/`HSAVE`/`HLOAD` persist/serve dispatch + raw-record sector I/O stays hardware-gated (the q16/i93/i95 gate). | `docs/specs/netboot-storage-manifest-design.md` §6.5; i93, i95, i99, i70, i100, i101, i119, i122; q16, q26 |
| **i115** | **Registry validator + format/order linter** (idea from the 2026-06-18 registry-as-DB discussion; Pete chose markdown-stays-source-of-truth + a validator over a shared DB). A check — a CLI `validate` + a CI required job — over `item-registry-{open,closed}.md` + `question-registry-{open,closed}.md` enforcing: **ids globally unique** across both halves; **numeric ordering** (catches the `i4, i41, i5` lexicographic slip); **well-formed table rows** (column count + the `\| **iN** \| … \|` shape); and **status token from the controlled vocabulary** (`OPEN`/`BLOCKED:<what>` in open files, `DONE`/`WONTFIX` in closed). Optional companions: a CLI that **allocates the next free id + emits a correctly-formatted row**, and an **exact-string row-edit helper** (find a cell between unique `iN`/`qN` markers and replace it in place, no full re-read) — 3+ sessions hand-rolled the latter as Python boundary-marker scripts (i104 retro Pattern: the multi-KB single-line cells are token-expensive to read under the Edit-before-write gate, and whole-line deletes are newline-fragile — both recur), so a named tool removes the per-session re-derivation + the Read-before-Edit tax. Why not a shared DB (the rejected alternative): it moves tracking out of git, breaking the PR-coupled / §3-reviewed / history-is-the-archive model; the orchestrator already supplies the parallelism, and the DB's only unique win (transactional concurrent inserts) is not needed at this scale. **Load-bearing rationale (Pete, 2026-06-18) — bigger than parallelism:** the registries are a *chained-coherence guarantee* — every agent edit carries a small `p(malform)` that **compounds** over hundreds of sessions toward eventual corruption (`1-(1-p)^N → 1`). A validator does not lower `p`; it makes a malformed edit **rejected at the iteration that introduces it** (a *required CI check* = enforced schema conformance), converting compounding-decay into a self-correcting system (double-entry-bookkeeping logic: the check runs every transaction). This is what keeps **markdown-as-source-of-truth viable at scale**: md *alone* (unenforced) is at its reliability limit over hundreds of iterations; md **+ an enforced schema/conformance gate** is the sweet spot — git-native (PR-coupled, §3-reviewed, history-as-archive, grep-able) *and* decay-proof — which is why this is the right answer rather than leaving git for a DB. Necessary-but-not-sufficient groundwork *if* parallel-writer agents are ever pursued (the original motivation, now secondary to this coherence-at-scale one). | OPEN — 🔨 umbrella; design AGREED, spec + plan landed; **model revised 2026-06-19** — no `BLOCKED` (it is derived from `depends_on` edges), questions transient (no closed view), the dependency DAG is CORE; **i115f reshape runs FIRST**; i115a/i115b were built on the old schema → need rework. Source-of-truth = structured YAML generating the markdown views, gated by a validator + CI sync-check. See `docs/specs/registry-source-of-truth-design.md`. | `docs/specs/registry-source-of-truth-design.md`; `docs/plans/registry-structured-source.md`; ROADMAP handover-contract rule 1 + the controlled-status vocabulary; i114; i117 |
