| **i118** | **i82 TFTP client — OACK handling / standard-TFTP-server interop** (found 2026-06-18 during Pete's i82 hardware test). The shipped i82 client (Go authority `tftp/clientloop.go`+`client/client.go` and the Z80 `netboot_client.asm`) is **not RFC 2347 compliant**: it requests RRQ options (blksize/tsize/timeout/windowsize) but has **no OACK (opcode 6) handling on either side**. A standards-compliant server answers an *optioned* RRQ with an **OACK before any DATA** (confirmed: macOS tftpd OACKs `blksize 1428 tsize <n> timeout 2 windowsize 4`); the client ignores it, never ACKs block 0, and **hangs**. The i82 host test masked this by injecting DATA directly (the OACK→ACK-0 handshake was never exercised), so i82's "host-verified end-to-end / DONE" overstated real-server interop. **Fix (branch `i82-tftp-client-oack`):** on OACK → learn the server endpoint + adopt the negotiated blksize (512 default if absent) + send ACK block 0; on direct DATA (no-options server) → the 512 default (today's hard-coded 1428 mis-detects the short-final after one 512-byte block → truncation); **drop `windowsize` from the request** (asking for it obliges RFC 7440 windowed delivery this lock-step receiver doesn't do → i120). Mirrored in the Go authority + host tests (OACK flow + 512 fallback now covered), then one real-hardware test vs Pete's Mac tftpd. | OPEN — 🔨 in progress (Go authority + Z80 + host tests on `i82-tftp-client-oack`; hardware test pending) | `tools/netboot-oracle/{tftp/tftp.go,tftp/clientloop.go,client/client.go}`; `src/netboot/netboot_client.asm`; `src/netboot/tftp_client_front.asm`; the host tests; i82 (corrects its interop gap), i119, i120 |
| **i120** | **Windowed TFTP (RFC 7440) for the netboot client/server — throughput optimization** (deferred from i118, 2026-06-18). i118 drops `windowsize` from the client RRQ to stay a correct lock-step (one ACK per block) client; that costs round-trips on large transfers (an 800 KB `.mgt` at blksize 1428 ≈ 574 lock-step round-trips over the SAM's slow ~20–80 KB/s SPI stack). Windowed delivery (window 4 → ~4× fewer round-trips) is the throughput win for the disk-image use case. Requires the receiver to ACK only the last block of each window (and handle lost-block/out-of-window rewind) on both client (i82) and, symmetrically, server (i83/i95); re-add `windowsize` to `ClientOptionSet` only once the windowed logic exists on both sides + is host-verified. | OPEN — 🔨 future optimization; not needed for correctness | `tools/netboot-oracle/tftp/`; `src/netboot/netboot_client.asm`; `docs/notes/tftp-protocol-research.md` (RFC 7440); i118, i82, i83, i95 |
| **i121** | **SAM TFTP server — WRQ (write / accept-in) support: push disk images to the SAM, auto-slotted** (Pete's PRIMARY netboot use case, 2026-06-18). The ergonomic "no SD-card dance" flow: boot the SAM, `tftp put` a disk image from the Mac, the SAM writes it to the next free Trinity record, then boot+test it immediately. This is the SAM as a TFTP **server accepting a WRQ** (RFC 1350 write request) — the opposite direction from the i82 client (RRQ/pull, which needs the filename configured SAM-side). A standard TFTP server handles both RRQ (serve-out) and WRQ (accept-in), so this **combines with the existing i83/i95 serve-out server into one program** (Pete's "both capabilities in parallel"). Reuses the i82 receive loop (DATA accumulate + ACK) + the i119 storage write-out; adds WRQ parsing + the server-side OACK / ACK-0 (the i83/i95 server already builds OACKs for serve-out) + **auto-slot allocation** (the i114 manifest / highest-free strategy). Net inbound/outbound story: rpi firmware pulled in over HTTP/HTTPS (i70/i88); disk images pushed in over TFTP-WRQ (this); images served out over TFTP-RRQ (i83/i95). | OPEN — 🔨 design (primary use case); foundation = i82 receive loop + i119 persistence + i114 allocation | `src/netboot/` (a WRQ path alongside netboot_serve/server); `tools/netboot-oracle/tftp/` (WRQ parse + server OACK); i82, i83, i95, i114, i119, i70 |
| **i122** | **SAM fetch-and-boot (PXE-style): TFTP-client-fetch a disk image and boot it directly** (Pete, 2026-06-18 — case 3 of the netboot taxonomy). The SAM acts like a netbooting Pi: on boot it TFTP-fetches a *named* disk image from a server (the server maintainer is responsible for serving the right image for that SAM — "I need SAM.mgt to boot"), writes it to a **scratch / boot record**, and boots into it — not necessarily persisting it (the disk is the thing it wants to RUN, not keep). Distinct from i82 (fetch + HSAVE to store) by the boot-into step: control does not return after boot, so the image must land on a bootable record first. **Design notes (Pete):** (a) it can't clean up post-boot (no control return) → use a reusable scratch record; (b) **sha256-dedup** — before fetching, check whether a disk with that content hash is already in storage; if so skip the fetch and boot the existing copy (idempotent, saves the transfer). The dedup reuses i114/q26's **content-addressed (hash-derived) record naming**. Builds on the i82 receive loop + the i119 write-out + a boot-a-record primitive (the Trinity RECORD-then-BOOT model). | OPEN — 🔨 design (case 3); foundation = i82 fetch + i119 write-out + i114 content-addressing | `src/netboot/netboot_client.asm`; `docs/notes/netboot-trinity-testing.md`; i82, i114, i119 |
| **i123** | **Investigate migrating the build from `make` to `tup`** (Pete, 2026-06-18 — prompted directly by the stale-`build/asmparse.bin` false alarm). `tup` tracks the full dependency graph and rebuilds *exactly* what is stale (and removes orphaned outputs), structurally preventing the dirty-build / stale-artifact class that bit us here — a test read a `.bin` older than its source and reported a misleading csneg failure. That is the **same class i116 patches test-by-test**; tup would make stale-`.bin` reads impossible *by construction* rather than asserted per-test, largely subsuming i116. Pete's `~/git/spectrum4` uses tup for exactly this reason ("amazing", "not too complicated to migrate"). Scope: evaluate tup against the current Makefile for this repo's rules (pyz80 `.asm`→`.bin`, the Go builds, disk-image assembly, the CI jobs), then if it wins, plan the migration. | OPEN — 🔨 investigation (Pete-flagged); compare vs current make, then decide | `Makefile`; `~/git/spectrum4` (tup precedent); i116 (the stale-bin class tup structurally fixes), i104 |
| **i124** | **Emulate the full Trinity boot path so the netboot *boot wrappers* run end-to-end in the harness (close the emulate-before-hardware gap)** (Pete, 2026-06-18 — after `client_main` shipped to hardware un-emulated and wasted a debugging session). **Concrete first target to reproduce:** the bootable client (record 13), even after the i119/i125 section-C fix, transmits **nothing** on the wire (hardware-confirmed 2026-06-18 via a wired-Pi raw-socket capture — only the router's ARP appears, *no* SAM frame under any MAC) → it hangs in `client_main` **before** `client_first`. The init path (EEPROM read → `drv_init`) is **byte-identical to the proven `smoke_main`**, and `drv_init`/`client_first` pass individually in the harness — so the bug is either a `client_main` logic detail or a **hardware-specific interaction** (the client's *proactive* ENC TX vs smoke's *reactive* sends, or EEPROM timing). Build this emulation to reproduce/localize it; **if `client_main` runs clean in emulation (reaches `client_first` + emits the ARP), the bug is hardware-specific** → fall back to minimal targeted instrumentation (border-colour markers through `client_main`/`drv_init`/`client_first`), NOT more blind SD-shuffling. The Go/koron-z80 harness already stubs the **LAN** (ENC28J60, `enc28j60.go`) and exercises the phase machines + `drv_init`, but the **boot entry points** (`client_main`/`smoke_main`/`server_main`) are compiled out (`ifndef NETBOOT_HOSTTEST`) because the harness has no **EEPROM config read** (`eeprom.asm` excluded) or **SD/B-DOS storage** (RST-8 HSAVE/HLOAD/HRECORD excluded). Stub those two (the "Trinity Network" config chunk + the record store) so the *whole* boot wrapper runs in emulation — catching logic bugs + enabling **autonomous iteration with no SD-card shuffling**. **Also model the SAM memory map (Pete, 2026-06-18):** mark **ROM1 read-only at `&C000` at boot** + LMPR/HMPR paging in the harness, so loading an **over-size or mis-paged** boot image **reproduces the real crash in emulation** (the flat all-RAM harness has no ROM1 there, so the program-vs-ROM conflict is invisible — which is exactly why this bug slipped through). **Reuse what exists:** the assembler harness `tools/z80-test-harness-go/harness.go` ALREADY models LMPR/HMPR paging (OUT-driven) — reuse that machinery; it just maps section D to **RAM page 3** (the assembler pages RAM there), so the *specific* missing piece is **ROM1-read-only-at-`&C000`-at-boot** (which neither the assembler nor the flat netboot harness models — which is exactly why this slipped through). Lightweight first cut: mark `&C000+` non-writable at boot (catches the over-size class, no ROM contents needed); fuller version loads the SAM ROM so the boot wrappers' ROM calls (`RST 16` print, `SETSTRM`, `CLSBL`) run too. (i125's fit-check is the cheap immediate guard for the same class; this gives the faithful in-emulator catch + *logic* coverage + removes the manual hardware loop.) Confirm the EEPROM + SD stubs are the last missing Trinity pieces. | OPEN — 🔨 boot-path emulation landed (PR #423): EEPROM modeled (`eeprom.go` from the real `eeprom.asm`) + ROM1-read-only-at-`&C000` (`LoadBoot`/`RunBoot`); `smoke_main` (control, reply == Go authority) + `client_main` run end-to-end in the harness. **i82 hang localized:** `client_main`'s logic is sound (reaches `client_first`, emits the ARP), so the fault is the real-silicon layer the model idealizes — root cause = **proactive ARP TX before PHY link-up + no ARP retransmit** (→ i127); smoke escapes it by being reactive. **Remaining:** model PHY link-up (LSTAT + drop-TX-while-down) to *reproduce* the drop in emulation; SD/RST-8 stub for the full client fetch; fold server/serve/http wrappers + delete the carve-outs (i126). | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/{eeprom,harness,enc28j60,netboot_boot_test}.go`; `src/netboot/eeprom.asm`, `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm`; i119, i125, i126, i127, i93 |
| **i126** | **One faithful SAM emulation layer, used by every test — fix the fragmented/inadequate emulation** (Pete, 2026-06-18; structural enforcement of CLAUDE.md discipline **rule 7**). Root cause of the i82 hardware-debug detour: **three partial emulators** with gaps the netboot boot path fell straight through — the **flat 64 KB netboot harness** (`tools/netboot-oracle/z80`: no paging/ROM/EEPROM), the **full assembler harness** (`tools/z80-test-harness-go/harness.go`: has LMPR/HMPR paging but maps section D to **RAM**, never models **ROM1-at-`&C000`-at-boot**), and **SimCoupé** (faithful SAM but **no Trinity** ENC/EEPROM/SD). So `client_main` was `NETBOOT_HOSTTEST`-excluded and tested only on hardware. **Deliverable:** consolidate to **one** emulation layer that (a) distinguishes **ROM vs RAM** + models **ROM1 read-only at `&C000` at boot** (cheap first cut: mark `&C000+` non-writable pre-paging — the exact gap that hid the over-size crash), (b) carries the **Trinity seams** (ENC28J60 — already stubbed — + the EEPROM config chunk + the SD/B-DOS record store), (c) is **used by every test** (fold the flat harness into a *mode* of the one layer, not a parallel harness), (d) runs the netboot **boot wrappers** end-to-end so the `NETBOOT_HOSTTEST` carve-outs are **deleted, not maintained**. Reuse harness.go's existing paging. Subsumes i124 (its EEPROM/ROM stub is the netboot slice of this). | OPEN — 🔨 architecture (Pete-flagged, high-level) | `tools/z80-test-harness-go/harness.go` (paging to reuse); `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/` (flat harness to fold in); `src/netboot/*` (`NETBOOT_HOSTTEST` carve-outs to delete); CLAUDE.md discipline rule 7; `feedback_emulation_first`; i124, i125, i116 |
| **i129** | **trinload stage-one bootstrap disk + bring-up flow (de-risk the disk-shipping top priority; Pete, 2026-06-18).** Build a bootable `trinload.mgt` (Simon Owen's trinload, `~/git/trinload`, BSD-ish — the same source `encdrv.asm`/`eeprom.asm` are vendored from) so a Trinity record can boot it as a proven, *reactive* stage-one bootloader that network-loads a code file into **ephemeral RAM** and runs it — no record write, no SD-card dance. Two uses: (a) load + run our netboot tools (e.g. the TFTP server) over the wire for **dev iteration** without re-flashing a record each time; (b) the ready **fallback transport** if our own client/server stalls — trinload loads our code, which then does the fetch-and-persist. NB: trinload runs code in RAM; persisting disk images to records still needs our i119 write-out — trinload removes the SD dance for *getting our code running*, it does not replace the persistence goal. Building the `.mgt` is host-autonomous; **writing it to a Trinity record + booting it is Pete's hardware step** (the SD card). Open Q: whether to vendor `trinload.asm` in-repo (consistent with the existing encdrv/eeprom vendoring) or keep the `.mgt` a local build artifact. | OPEN — 🔨 **`.mgt` built + WRITTEN to Trinity record 128** (2026-06-19, agent over SSH; guard-confirmed empty first; verified `samdos2`+`AUTOtrin.O`). ✅ push→run→return HARDWARE-CONFIRMED 2026-06-19 (code.bin pushed via `trinload3.py` → border cycled → trinload survived); also on rec 3 (identical image). Effectively DONE; vendor-in-repo Q SETTLED by i132 (trinload.asm vendored at `src/netboot/trinload.asm`); move to closed at next sweep. | `~/git/trinload/trinload.asm`; `tools/build-disk/`; i82, i119, i121, i130; the disk-shipping top priority |
| **i130** | **Name every unnamed-but-occupied Trinity record so none gets accidentally wiped (Pete, 2026-06-19).** The card (Atom Lite, 65535 records) holds Pete's whole collection — records 10→817+, file counts 1-80 — but **most occupied records are UNNAMED** (present via `samdisk dir`, absent from `samdisk list`), so a `list`-gap looks free when it isn't (the documented gotcha). Goal: give each occupied-but-unnamed record a label (Pete's spec: `L<rec>`) so `list` shows it. **Mechanism BLOCKER:** `samdisk` 3.8.8 has no name/label subcommand — a record's name comes from the disk *image* written to it, so naming needs the SAM/Atom disk-name storage format pinned down (research the `~/git/samdisk` *current checkout* — older non-head version matching 3.8.8; head is broken-on-mac alpha) then a careful per-record **read-modify-write** (back up each record image first; **test on ONE** + verify files intact + the name shows in `list`; only THEN the Python bulk loop). High-stakes — irreplaceable collection; do NOT bulk-write on an unvalidated mechanism. Empty records found (safe to use, do NOT need naming): 123, 128 (=trinload), 145, 171, 172. | ✅ DONE 2026-06-19 — 805 records named (method validated on rec 128 first, then Python `L<rec>` bulk over 1-850, 0 data failures, all verified). Cosmetic: ~424 show a trailing leftover char (samdisk copy writes only the 10-byte label; harmless — record is named+visible). Move to closed-registry at next sweep | `~/git/samdisk` (source, current checkout); SAMdisk 3.8.8; [[trinity_hardware_first_light]]; i129 |
| **i133** | **Self-service autonomous hardware testing via trinload (Pete's vision, 2026-06-19).** With trinload running on the SAM (rec 3/128) and control returning between programs (i132 confirms the mechanism), run unattended experiments: push a program over the wire (`trinload3.py`), it runs + `RET`s to trinload, push the next. Get feedback two ways: **(a) sniff the wire** (tcpdump on the Mac/Pi) for packets in/out, and **(b) a network result channel** — the test program writes its results/diagnostics back over the network (a "stdout over UDP/TCP", the hardware analogue of the printer-channel trick used in the emulator), so the agent can **debug/troubleshoot real-hardware issues directly, unattended**. Needs **RET-not-halt program variants** (our client `di;halt`s — make a trinload-friendly variant that re-inits the ENC at start, RETs to trinload at end, and emits results over the net) + i132 for pre-flight. A crash needs a Pete reboot until i135 (auto-boot + Tapo) closes that gap. Force-multiplier: the agent iterates on hardware unsupervised. | OPEN — 🔨 the operational autonomous-test loop; needs i132 + RET-able+net-reporting program variants | `~/git/trinload/test/trinload.py` (→ py3 `trinload3.py` on the Mac); `src/netboot/*`; i132, i135, i127 |
| **i135** | **Auto-boot a Trinity record on power-up → unsupervised end-to-end hardware automation (Pete, 2026-06-19).** Pete has a Tapo smart plug that can programmatically power-cycle the SAM. If a record can **auto-boot on power-on** (no manual record-select + typing `BOOT`), the agent runs fully unsupervised end-to-end hardware experiments: power-cycle → SAM boots a chosen record → runs → results over the net (i133) → power-cycle for the next. Investigate the means: a config setting (Pete recalls a code path checking an EEPROM/config setting for a default boot disk/record — find it), the **Trinity AutoBOOT ROM** (Colin Piggot's patch loads B-DOS from the 128K EEPROM at reset), or **patch the forked B-DOS** for an early boot-a-record hook. Research: `~/sam-archive/bdos/analysis` (B-DOS RECORD/boot), the Trinity AutoBOOT ROM, the EEPROM layout, `~/sam-archive/trinity-docs`. Pairs with i133 for the full unsupervised loop. | OPEN — 🔨 investigation (Pete-flagged, high-leverage); enables unsupervised hardware testing | `~/sam-archive/bdos/analysis`; the Trinity AutoBOOT ROM (i87); EEPROM config; `~/sam-archive/trinity-docs`; i133, i87 |
| **i137** | **`build-disk`'s netboot ceiling check doesn't distinguish section class (i125 follow-up, found 2026-06-19).** `tools/build-disk/main.go:516` rejects a netboot image only if it loads past the `&10000` ceiling (≤32768 bytes) — a single budget for all. But a *section-C* bootable program (smoke/serve/server/client) must fit `&8000-&BFFF` (≤16384); above that it loads into ROM1 at boot and crashes (the i119 class). So a 16385–32768-byte section-C image passes `build-disk` but would crash on hardware. **Mostly masked today:** the per-program `make netboot-*-boot` fit-check (i125, `netboot-boot-fit-check.sh`, 16384 for section-C / 32768 for the http overlay) fires *first* in the normal `make netboot-*-disk` flow, so an over-section-C binary never reaches `build-disk`. The gap is only reachable by calling `build-disk -netboot <bin>` directly on an over-section-C image. Fix would need `build-disk` to know each image's section class (a `-netboot-section-c` flag threaded through the disk targets) so its last-line-of-defence check matches the make gate. Low priority (masked); tracked so it isn't lost. | OPEN — 🔨 minor robustness (masked by the i125 make gate) | `tools/build-disk/main.go` (the `&10000` netboot check); `tools/netboot-boot-fit-check.sh`; i125, i119 |
| **i139** | **Autonomous-loop monitor: `CONTEXT_SETTLE` 5s too short → the task-done resume nudge is dropped** (Pete, 2026-06-19; follow-up to i136/#428). After `touch task-done`, the monitor stuffs `/context`, waits `CONTEXT_SETTLE`, then stuffs the `RESUME_NUDGE` (the continue-vs-wind-down decision rule). `/context` is a full-TUI redraw; with the settle at 5s the nudge keystrokes land mid-render and are dropped, so no next turn fires and the agent sits idle until the ~1h hang-timeout (observed twice this session — `/context` rendered, no nudge followed). Fix: bump `CONTEXT_SETTLE` default 5→30 (matches the proven `CLEAR_SETTLE`); the comment already called it "the /context analogue of CLEAR_SETTLE". **A running monitor must be restarted to apply** (or relaunch with `ALOOP_CONTEXT_SETTLE=30`). Superseded by i140: the longer sleep did not fix it — the real cause was burst-size, not a render race. | OPEN — 🔨 fix in PR; needs monitor restart to take effect | `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh` (`CONTEXT_SETTLE`); i136 (#428, added the settle); `tools/autonomous-loop/README.md`; i140 |
| **i140** | **monitor.sh task-done nudge not delivered — the second message after `/context` is dropped** (Pete, 2026-06-19, after i139). On TASK_DONE the monitor stuffs `/context` then (after `CONTEXT_SETTLE`) `RESUME_NUDGE`, but the nudge never becomes a turn, so the loop stalls at the checkpoint until the ~1h hang-timeout. i139 bumped `CONTEXT_SETTLE` 5→30 (needs a monitor RESTART) but the symptom persists. Root cause (reproduced in an isolated test screen session, never the live one): a long string stuffed as ONE `screen -X stuff` burst right after `/context`'s full-TUI redraw is dropped wholesale (0/4 delivered) — not a render race a longer sleep can win. Fixed: `stuff` now chunks every payload into small bursts, and the task-done branch uses `nudge_until_turn` (verify-and-retry — polls the screen for the `esc to interrupt` turn marker). Verified end-to-end by running the modified monitor against a fresh isolated session (`nudge delivered (attempt 1, 1s)`, agent took a real turn, twice). | OPEN — 🔨 root-caused + fixed; verified in isolated session | `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh`; `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor-nudge-delivery.md`; `tools/autonomous-loop/README.md`; i139, i97 |
